"BWIGLEY" <bwigley@xtra.co.nz> wrote:

[Best? Worst?]

My favorite parts of NetHack:

  names returned when ;-IDing coyotes
  patience games like credit cloning, altar camping,
    pudding farming, exploring mazes (especially
    dark ones), solving Sokoban, or playing levels
    blind

My least favorite parts of NetHack:

  monsters that damage and/or destroy armor
  losing important stuff and not noticing for
    several levels that it's gone
  my uncontrollable YASDism
  
I'm currently playing a series of healers, learning
how to take advantage of their incredible early
features, like starting with 25-28 HPs after
quaffing all their healing potions, like early
self-and-pet-healing, and like instant satiation
from creating huge chunks of meat, by the time
they've gotten to XL 2 or 3, versus their huge
initial disadvantages, like wimpy starting armor, a
worthless weapon, and horrible strength.

So far I've gotten one to seventh place on my
personal score table.

The protection racket doesn't work well, but
credit cloning with a huge starting stack of
zorkmids and a charisma of 16-18 is just awesome.
Often I can bankrupt a shopkeeper in one iterated
buy-sell cycle.

The best usually available weapon I've found so far
is a dwarven mattock, which doesn't hit very often
at unskilled, but often does a one hit kill for
early game monsters, but it's way heavy for so
strengthless a PC. Supplemented with a stack of
daggars or even darts (thanks to whomever recently
pointed out the utility of darts), weaponry problems
are fairly easy to overcome.

Wearing next to no armor and just running away a
lot, and caring for buff pets, seems to work better
than getting burdened to gain good AC.

    [Except for the healer who found +1 crystal
    plate mail just laying around, and was able to
    cast four spells with zero failure rate with an
    AC around 0 very early in the game; being
    burdened wasn't all that bad in that situation.]

But despite several gauntlets of strength, usable
altars, even Vorpal Blade found in a plain room atop
a trap door one time, I can't seem to complete
Sokoban or Mines before I do something awesomely
stupid, like starving in Sokoban, or forgetting to
cast healing on myself in a melee I can't outrun.

Sigh.

